# arvo-graph — backlog

> **Type-surface reminder**: new entries use `arvo::USize` /
> `Bool` / `UFixed<N, 0, S>` instead of `usize` / `bool` / `u*`;
> `notko::Maybe` / `Outcome` instead of `Option` / `Result`. See
> `rules/type-surface.md`.

Round 202605031748 (#313) collapsed the BitMatrix family onto
`BitMatrix<W, const N: Cap>` chassis. Algorithms ship today against
`W = Bits<64, Hot, Unsigned>` for up to 64 nodes; instantiating the
same algorithms with `W = Bits<256, Hot, Unsigned>` extends to 256
nodes via the substrate's MultiContainer plumbing without an
algorithmic-shape change.

## Wider-W ergonomic helpers

Common pipeline workloads per polka-dots research stay well under 64
nodes, so the 64-wide instantiation covers the common case. When a
consumer surfaces a concrete need for >64-node adjacency, the
algorithm bodies require zero changes; only the call-site
instantiation flips from `BitMatrix<Bits<64, Hot, Unsigned>, N>` to
`BitMatrix<Bits<256, Hot, Unsigned>, N>`. Cookbook documentation of
the wider-W instantiation pattern is the only deferred work.
